# Localization, instantons, and BPS states

Research note for the proposed **Advanced QFT and supersymmetry** category,
dated 2026-10-23. This note covers finite-dimensional localization, its use in
supersymmetric field theory, S4/S3 partition-function calculations, instanton
counting, solitons/monopoles, BPS indices, quiver wall crossing, and spectral
networks. The category itself is new; the repository already contains related
mathematical and QFT material described below. This is a research scaffold, not
a production item set, a publication decision, or a claim that every cited
formula has a rigorous continuum construction.

Every technical term in a future carrier needs a precise definition or an exact
supplier interface. A source citation is not a substitute for that definition
or for a proof. Use mathematical items only for mathematical premises. Physical
theorems and thought experiments must be complete conditional deductions from
explicit model assumptions. A source-reported formal field-theory calculation
must remain labelled formal unless its functional measure, regulator, Ward
identity and limiting argument have actually been constructed and proved.
Open-problem candidates below are not suppliers.

## Existing interfaces and reuse boundaries

The following are exact current page and item identities. “Status” refers to
the current carrier/frontmatter read on 2026-10-23. Where metadata and the body
give different review signals, the note records the discrepancy instead of
silently treating a `published` label as proof acceptance.

| Interface | Existing page and exact items | Reuse boundary |
|---|---|---|
| Compact Lie groups, torus actions and symplectic geometry | `library/differential-geometry/moment-maps-and-symplectic-reduction.md` (`moment-maps-and-symplectic-reduction`): `def-symplectic-and-hamiltonian-lie-group-action`, `def-moment-map-and-component-hamiltonian`, `prop-moment-map-components-generate-the-negative-infinitesimal-action`, `thm-marsden-weinstein-meyer-symplectic-reduction`, `prop-compact-group-symplectic-actions-admit-an-invariant-compatible-almost-complex-structure`. The page also contains `rem-convexity-and-toric-classification-for-hamiltonian-torus-actions`. | Supplies smooth actions, moment maps, symplectic reduction and compatible almost-complex structures. It does **not** define Borel/Cartan equivariant cohomology or prove the Atiyah–Bott–Berline–Vergne fixed-point formula. The convexity remark names equivariant cohomology as adjacent context, not as a proved supplier. |
| Ordinary Dirac index and characteristic forms | `library/differential-geometry/spin-geometry-dirac-and-index-theory.md` (`spin-geometry-dirac-and-index-theory`): `def-graded-hermitian-clifford-module-and-geometric-dirac`, `lem-dirac-maximal-domain-is-h1`, `lem-chiral-dirac-is-fredholm-with-kernel-and-cokernel`, `thm-geometric-clifford-module-dirac-index-formula`, `cor-spin-dirac-index-formula-with-arbitrary-twist`, and `def-ahat-and-relative-chern-character-forms`. Also `library/differential-geometry/chern-weil-theory-and-characteristic-forms.md` (`chern-weil-theory-and-characteristic-forms`): `def-invariant-polynomial-on-a-matrix-lie-algebra`, `def-evaluation-of-an-invariant-polynomial-on-curvature`, `thm-chern-weil-homomorphism-is-independent-of-connection-and-natural`, and `thm-chern-weil-forms-represent-the-at-characteristic-classes-over-the-reals`. | These supply ordinary closed-manifold Fredholm/index and characteristic-form interfaces, with their compactness, smoothness, boundaryless and AC hypotheses. They supply neither equivariant characters nor a regularized infinite product of fluctuation eigenvalues. **Status caveat:** the Dirac page and theorem carrier say `published`, but the theorem's visible verification field has only `precheck: pass`; the page body says the local construction and full index proofs remain drafts for independent review. Treat it as context, not an accepted logical premise, until the owner resolves that inconsistency. The twisted corollary has a judge field but likewise no visible `verified`/`audited` field in the current carrier. Do not repair root mathematics here. |
| ASD gauge-theory index | `items/thm-asd-deformation-complex-index-formula.md` (`thm-asd-deformation-complex-index-formula`), with exact operator `D_A=d_A^*\oplus d_A^-` on a closed oriented Riemannian four-manifold and formula `8k-3(1-b_1+b_-)`. The current carrier has a delegated `verified` stamp dated 2026-10-22. | A useful ordinary Fredholm-index supplier for the ASD deformation complex on a **closed** four-manifold. It is not an equivariant index theorem, does not classify framed R4 instantons, and does not prove the Nekrasov or S4 functional-integral formula. |
| Principal bundles and gauge transformations | `library/lie-theory/gauge-groups-and-representation-theory-interfaces.md` (`gauge-groups-and-representation-theory-interfaces`): `def-smooth-principal-g-bundle`, `def-principal-connection-and-vertical-curvature`, `def-gauge-group-of-a-smooth-principal-bundle`, `prop-gauge-transformations-act-on-principal-connections`, and `prop-gauge-transformations-induce-associated-bundle-automorphisms`. | Supplies finite-dimensional bundle/connection/gauge-action language. It does not define a measure on the quotient of an infinite-dimensional space of connections. State the global form of the gauge group, the bundle, boundary/framing conditions, and quotient in every instanton application. |
| Finite-quiver stability and Hall wall crossing | `library/hall-algebras-and-tensor-categories/hall-stability-dt-bps-and-wall-crossing.md` (`hall-stability-dt-bps-and-wall-crossing`): `def-finite-acyclic-quiver-stability-data`, `lem-finite-acyclic-quiver-representations-are-finitary-hereditary-with-euler-form`, `lem-upper-half-plane-sum-phase-between`, `lem-quiver-hardernarasimhan-filtrations-exist-are-unique-and-descend`, `def-subobject-first-completed-finite-quiver-hall-algebra-and-tori`, `thm-finite-quiver-euler-and-skew-integration-is-multiplicative`, `thm-finite-quiver-hall-wall-crossing`, and `rem-finite-quiver-hall-counts-and-dt-bps-interpretation`. | This is a precise finite-field, finite-acyclic-quiver Hall/HN construction; its source page explicitly says it is not Behrend-weighted CY3 DT, motivic vanishing-cycle integration, cohomological BPS spaces or physical state counting. The theorem carrier is tagged `published` and contains a local proof, but the visible frontmatter has only `verification.precheck: pass`; bind the actual acceptance evidence before treating the current file as a certified proof supplier. |
| QFT-local Hall spectrum generator | `physics/library/quantum-field-theory-mathematics/qftx-hall-bps-wall-crossing.md` (`qftx-hall-bps-wall-crossing`) and its examples page `qftx-hall-bps-wall-crossing-examples`. Exact items are `def-qftx-hall-spectrum-generator`, `prop-qftx-hall-spectrum-invariance`, and `def-qftx-cy3-bps-integration-contract`, all `domain: mathematics`, `library: mathematics`. The generator is defined for finite acyclic quivers over `F_q`; the invariance proposition uses `thm-finite-quiver-hall-wall-crossing`; the bridge item is a definition of required input data. The first two items have delegated verification dated 2026-10-22; the bridge is also source-reviewed/verified in that run. | This is the strongest exact reuse interface for the new category's quiver wall-crossing prose. `P_Z^E` and `P_Z^sk` are formal products in a dimension completion; no analytic convergence or physical state-counting is asserted. `def-qftx-cy3-bps-integration-contract` lists the extra CY3/category, stability, orientation, completed Hall/CoHA integration and physical Hilbert-supertrace comparison required for any bridge. It explicitly does not assert that such a package exists or that arbitrary Hall modules are BPS spaces. |
| Quiver CoHA and motivic constructions | `physics/library/hall-algebras-and-tensor-categories/cohomological-hall-algebras-and-quiver-correspondences.md` (`cohomological-hall-algebras-and-quiver-correspondences`): `def-quiver-representation-stacks-and-exact-sequence-correspondences`, `thm-quiver-quotient-stack-borel-moore-operation-package`, `def-untwisted-quiver-cohomological-hall-algebra`, `thm-untwisted-quiver-coha-is-associative-and-unital`, `def-quiver-potential-and-monodromic-vanishing-cycle-data`, `def-critical-quiver-coha-from-vanishing-cycle-data`, `thm-critical-quiver-coha-associative-under-coherence-axioms`, and related lemmas. Also `physics/library/hall-algebras-and-tensor-categories/motivic-hall-algebras-and-integration.md` (`motivic-hall-algebras-and-integration`): `def-motivic-hall-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver`, `def-quiver-motivic-quantum-torus`, `thm-quiver-motivic-hall-convolution-associative`, `thm-quiver-motivic-integration-is-multiplicative`. | These pages define equivariant quotient-stack Borel–Moore operations and finite-quiver Hall constructions. They are specialized to the stated quotient stacks, coefficient systems and operations; the CoHA page explicitly does not identify them with physical BPS Hilbert spaces. The local CoHA page excludes mixed-Hodge enrichment and off-shell rapid-decay cohomology. This equivariant Borel–Moore theory is not the missing smooth-manifold Borel/Cartan theory or a general localization theorem. |
| Finite/regulator QFT mathematics | `physics/library/quantum-field-theory-mathematics/qft-ir01.md` (`qft-ir01`): `def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian`, `thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction`, `thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace`; `qft-ir02.md` (`qft-ir02`): `def-qft-finite-euclidean-interacting-measure`, `lem-qft-finite-gaussian-wick-moments`, `thm-qft-finite-euclidean-polynomial-measure`; `qftx-bv-brst.md` (`qftx-bv-brst`): `def-qftx-abelian-local-brst`, `lem-qftx-brst-nilpotence-doublets`, `def-qftx-brst-relative-local-cohomology`, `prop-qftx-anomaly-obstruction`; `qftx-anomaly-cocycles.md` (`qftx-anomaly-cocycles`): `def-qftx-loop-current-cocycle`, `prop-qftx-loop-cocycle-jacobi`, `def-qftx-chiral-anomaly-interface`. | `qft-ir01` and `qft-ir02` give exact fixed-finite-N mathematical models and explicitly make no continuum/field-measure claim. `qftx-bv-brst` is an Abelian local interface, not a non-Abelian supersymmetric localization complex. Reuse finite-regulator mathematics only after proving that the chosen regulator preserves the specific supercharge and has the necessary measure/limit properties. |
| QFT physical conventions | `physics/library/quantum-field-theory/qft-c01.md` (`qft-c01`): `def-qft-framework-units`, `post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement`; `physics/library/quantum-field-theory/qft-pir01.md` (`qft-pir01`): `post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model`, `def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter`, `pthm-qft-regulated-interacting-model-evolution`, `post-qft-regulated-equilibrium-state`, `pthm-qft-regulated-equilibrium-predictions`. | Reuse unit conventions and the distinction between adopted models and constructed finite regulators. These suppliers do not identify their bosonic regulator with supersymmetric S4/S3 theories. State natural/SI units, `\hbar`, `c`, radius, coupling, trace normalization and all suppressed constants in each new framework. |

Two near matches are not interchangeable with BPS interfaces. `def-equivariant-borel-moore-homology-and-correspondence-convolution` is a constructible equivariant BM construction for specified general-linear actions/correspondences, not equivariant de Rham cohomology of compact smooth manifolds. The EM example `physics/items/ex-emgmath-monopole-patch-flux.md` is a fixed-normalization Abelian flux calculation; it is not a non-Abelian Higgs monopole, a BPS solution or a quantum magnetic-charge measurement. Any neural-network item containing “spectral network” is unrelated to the Gaiotto–Moore–Neitzke construction.

## Smallest missing mathematical suppliers

The local mathematics should be built before a supersymmetric functional-integral
application. Candidate stable IDs below are proposed planning names, not minted
items. All are `domain: mathematics` with mathematical dependencies only.

| Candidate | Kind | Required statement and local proof obligations |
|---|---|---|
| `def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model` | definition | For a compact torus `T` acting smoothly on a manifold `M`, define the Borel space `ET×_T M`, `H_T^*(M;R)`, the coefficient ring `H_T^*(pt;R)=Sym(t^*)` with degree-two generators, and the Cartan complex `(Sym(t^*)⊗Ω(M))^T` with `d_T=d-Σu_i ι_{X_i}`. Prove `d_T^2=0` on invariants. If using the Cartan model as a computational substitute, prove/cite the comparison for the declared compact-connected group and coefficients; do not conflate rational Borel cohomology, real Cartan cohomology, equivariant Chow groups and quotient-stack Borel–Moore homology. |
| `thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions` | theorem | For a compact smooth `T`-manifold and rational or real coefficients, define the multiplicative set generated by nonzero characters of `T`. Prove restriction to the fixed locus `M^T` becomes an isomorphism after that localization, with its precise compactness/finite-type hypotheses. The proof needs the fixed-set submanifold, a `T`-invariant tubular neighborhood, the equivariant Thom/Gysin map and the fact that each normal weight is invertible in the localized coefficient ring. On the complement, give a finite orbit-type/slice cover; each proper isotropy subgroup has a nonzero character vanishing on its identity component, so its equivariant cohomology is torsion after localization. State separately any properness/compact-support version for noncompact spaces. |
| `thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula` | theorem | Under a compact oriented smooth `T`-manifold hypothesis and an equivariant orientation of each fixed component's normal bundle, prove `∫_M α = Σ_{F⊂M^T} ∫_F i_F^*α/e_T(ν_F)` in the localized coefficient ring. Define `i_F`, equivariant pushforward, the normal Euler class, integration and degree conventions. Derive the isolated-fixed-point specialization from the fixed-locus theorem; specify the complex orientation/sign and the torus weights. The local proof should use `i_F^*i_{F*}(β)=e_T(ν_F)β` and the localized inverse restriction, rather than invoke a path-integral saddle. It is not a theorem for arbitrary noncompact instanton spaces or for an infinite-dimensional field space. |
| `def-equivariant-elliptic-complex-index` | definition | For a compact group `G` acting on a closed manifold and a `G`-equivariant elliptic complex, define the finite-dimensional kernel/cokernel representations and `Ind_G(D)∈R(G)`; define its character and the fixed-element hypothesis. Separate elliptic from transversally elliptic operators and say when the latter have infinite representation sums. |
| `thm-equivariant-index-character-formula` | theorem | Supply the fixed-set character formula for the precisely chosen elliptic/transversely elliptic class. Prove that the action preserves the symbol and all coefficient bundles, derive the fixed-locus contribution with its normal denominator and characteristic classes, and specify evaluation away from singular group elements. `thm-geometric-clifford-module-dirac-index-formula` is only the ordinary index and cannot discharge this item. Before using it as a load-bearing supplier, resolve the current status discrepancy recorded in the table above. |
| `def-finite-dimensional-q-exact-localization-data` | definition | Define a finite-dimensional graded integral (including its Berezin variables if used), integration cycle, odd differential/vector field `Q`, square `Q^2`, measure, action, observable and admissible deformation. Require a `Q`-invariant cycle/measure, an explicit domain/contour, and either compact support or stated decay ensuring no boundary terms and differentiation under the integral. |
| `thm-finite-dimensional-q-exact-deformation-invariance` | theorem | For the preceding data let `Q` be odd, `V` odd, `S` and the observable `O` even, require `Q^2V=0` (or that `Q^2` acts trivially on the integration cycle/integrand), `QS=QO=0`, and a `Q`-invariant measure and cycle (`div_μ Q=0` in coordinates). Prove `dZ_t/dt=0` by differentiating `∫O e^{-S-tQV}` and integrating the resulting `Q`-exact divergence. State a uniform integrable majorant for the derivative on every compact `t`-interval so differentiation/interchange is justified, and prove the boundary term vanishes (compact support or explicit decay). A large-`t` saddle limit additionally requires a proper coercive bosonic term, a characterized zero set, nondegenerate normal Hessian or a specified degenerate analysis, treatment of zero modes and a justified asymptotic limit. The finite-dimensional theorem does not establish a continuum Ward identity. |
| `def-framed-u-n-instanton-moduli-and-adhm-data` | definition | Fix Euclidean `R^4` or its one-point compactification, orientation/Hodge star, a framed principal `U(N)` bundle, the trace normalization and instanton number, the gauge group at infinity, and the ASD/SD sign. Define the ADHM vector spaces/maps `(B_1,B_2,I,J)`, moment-map equations, stability condition, `U(k)` quotient, torus action and any nonzero FI/noncommutative resolution parameter. Distinguish the smooth resolved space from the singular small-instanton boundary and the Uhlenbeck/Gieseker compactifications. |
| `thm-adhm-fixed-point-instanton-count` | theorem | Prove the ADHM correspondence under the stated group, framing, stability and FI hypotheses; identify the fixed loci for `T^2×T_G`; prove the classification by `N`-tuples of partitions and compute the tangent/virtual-character weights. Apply the local finite-dimensional localization theorem only after specifying a legitimate equivariant pushforward or a compactification/proper fixed-locus condition. State whether the coefficient is a formal series in `q` or an analytic function. |

Possible follow-on local lemmas include the fixed-locus normal-weight calculation,
gauge-fixing/ghost cancellation for a **finite** regulator, a determinant-line
orientation lemma, and the one-loop superdeterminant identity for a specified
complex of modes. Do not predeclare these as consequences of the ordinary
Atiyah–Singer theorem. A character/index records a virtual kernel/cokernel; a
regularized product of all nonzero eigenvalues needs its own spectral, contour
and regularization argument. For every mathematical supplier in the eventual
plan, bind the exact item/page path, statement, hypotheses, current proof and
verification status, any Axiom-of-Choice strength, and the precise consumer
use. Keep all proposed mathematical items on mathematical-only dependency
edges; do not repair their root originals from this physics workspace.

## Supersymmetric backgrounds, path integrals, and S4/S3 formulas

Definitions needed before using these terms include: the dimension/signature and
orientation of spacetime; spin structure and spinor bundles; the gauge principal
bundle and connection; each matter representation and field statistics; the
off-shell multiplet and auxiliary fields; the R-symmetry and its background
connection; the supercharge(s) and their reality/normalization convention; and
the background Killing-spinor equations. A rigid supersymmetric background is
not merely a curved manifold: it is a metric plus specified background
supergravity/R-symmetry/auxiliary fields admitting a nonzero solution of the
chosen fermion-variation equations. State the spin structure, reality contour,
R-charges and background fields, and explicitly compute the square `Q^2` as the
combination of isometry, gauge, R-symmetry and flavor transformations used in
the localization complex. Do not assume every metric admits such a background.

A partition function must name its field space, action, integration cycle,
gauge quotient/gauge fixing, measure or regulator, boundary conditions, and
parameters. A formal symbol `∫DΦ e^{-S[Φ]}` is not a constructed measure.
The usual localization step adds `t QV` and differentiates in `t`. This is a
formal physics Ward-identity derivation unless the regulated measure is
Q-invariant, the integration cycle has no boundary contribution, the integral
converges sufficiently to differentiate, the gauge/ghost complex is defined,
and the regulator and limit preserve these properties. An anomaly, contour
deformation, noncompact zero-mode direction, or instanton boundary can invalidate
the step. Name each such assumption rather than hiding it in “supersymmetry
protects the answer.”

The first carriers also need explicit definitions for these objects and their
domains: a **rigid limit** (which background supergravity fields are frozen and
which Planck-scale limit is taken); a **supersymmetry complex** (graded fields,
odd operator `Q`, its domain and exact square `Q^2`); a **gauge-fixed fluctuation
operator** (bundles, Sobolev/domain, ghosts and stabilizers); a **one-loop
determinant** (the ratio of specified bosonic/fermionic spectral determinants,
regularization, phase and zero-mode convention); and a **functional-integral
regulator** (finite mode/lattice/cutoff space, measure, contour and removal
map). For physical formulas, state whether fields and couplings are in SI or
natural units and give every suppressed constant. Under the common `ħ=c=1`
choice, a 4D gauge coupling is dimensionless, `a`, masses and `ε_i` have
mass dimension one, the sphere radius `r` has dimension length, and the
instanton fugacity `q` is dimensionless. Restore units explicitly if a result
is compared with an SI observable.

The finite-dimensional theorem and the field-theory formula have different
domains. The rigorous cohomological localization formula applies to a
finite-dimensional compact manifold (or a separately proved compact-support /
proper-pushforward extension) with smooth torus action, equivariant class and
invertible normal Euler classes after localization. A finite-dimensional
supersymmetric integral also needs the explicit cycle, measure, Ward identity
and decay in `thm-finite-dimensional-q-exact-deformation-invariance`. The
continuum field-space version must separately construct the infinite-dimensional
measure, gauge fixing, determinants, zero modes, instanton sectors, convergence
or a regulator-removal limit. None of the local finite-dimensional theorems
proves that a continuum functional integral exists.

For the round S4 example, Pestun's field-theory derivation for specified 4D
`N=2` vector/hyper theories produces the structural expression

`Z_{S^4} = (1/|W|) ∫_{\mathfrak t} da\, e^{-8π^2 r^2(a,a)/g_{YM}^2}\, Z_{1-loop}(ia)\, |Z_{inst}(ia, ε_1=r^{-1}, ε_2=r^{-1}, q)|^2`,

up to the paper's measure, root and coupling conventions. Here `G` is the named
compact gauge group, `T` a chosen maximal torus, `W` its Weyl group, `r` the
sphere radius, `a` the constant Cartan scalar at the saddle, `Z_{1-loop}` the
gauge-fixed fluctuation determinant, and the north/south pole factors count
instantons and anti-instantons in opposite Ω-backgrounds. A carrier would need
to state the exact representation content, masses, coupling, contour and
normalization before asserting a formula. In these conventions `q=e^{2πiτ}` is
the dimensionless instanton parameter for the declared complexified coupling
`τ`; `ia` records the paper's contour/analytic-continuation convention, and
`(a,a)` uses its specified invariant quadratic form. The formula is a source-reported
formal field-theory result in this scaffold, not a proved continuum theorem.
Pestun explicitly says that the noncommutative S4 deformation is not explicitly
constructed in the paper and assumes it is well-defined and agrees with the
Nekrasov R4 deformation near each pole. That is a material condition, not a
minor footnote.

For a 3D `N=2` theory on the round S3, the Kapustin–Willett–Yaakov calculation
reduces its specified superconformal Chern–Simons–matter model to a Cartan
integral of the form

`Z_{S^3} = (1/|W|) ∫_{\mathfrak t} da\, e^{-S_{cl}(a)} Z^{vec}_{1-loop}(a) Z^{matter}_{1-loop}(a;R)`,

where the vector determinant includes the root/Vandermonde factor and the matter
determinant depends on the declared representations and R-charges `R`. Exact
signs, `2π` factors, level quantization, FI terms and contour depend on the
authors' convention and theory; read and reproduce that convention before
making a formula a local item. KWY's cited result is for superconformal
Chern–Simons–matter theories (including their explicit U(N) and ABJM cases),
not every 3D supersymmetric QFT. Again, it is a field-theoretic localization
calculation, not proof of a general continuum measure. The schematic integral
sets the sphere radius to one; when restoring a radius, state the rescaling of
the Cartan scalar and all dimensionful masses/FI parameters. State the integer
Chern–Simons level and the exact R-charge convention of each chiral multiplet.

The mathematical one-loop route should be split into separate obligations:
define the boson/fermion fluctuation complex and gauge-fixing ghosts; prove the
linearized `Q` complex and its symbol/ellipticity or transverse ellipticity;
identify actual zero modes and remove/gauge them with correct stabilizers;
compute its equivariant character/index under explicit hypotheses; derive the
nonzero-mode determinant ratio from a finite regulator or a stated spectral
regularization; check regularization-scale/anomaly terms; and normalize the
orientation/determinant line. Cite the exact `Q^2` action and ensure all
operators commute with the chosen symmetry. Do not infer the determinant from
the index alone.


