a^2X$. By [A1], $s^2X=sX'=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)X$. By [A3], the gauge term cancels with its negative in $\\{s,\\delta\\}X$, leaving $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)X+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)X$. [A1, A3, given, algebra]\n\n2.1 For the connection, the same square expansion applies to $A$ and $\\psi$. By [A1], $s^2A=s\\psi=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)A+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)A$; by [A2] and [A3], its BRST rule is $\\delta A_\\mu=D_\\mu c=-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(c)A_\\mu$, its mixed term cancels the $\\phi$ action, and its BRST square is $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)A$. Thus $Q^2A=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)A+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)A$, using the declared connection gauge action $-D_\\mu u$. [A1, A2, A3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 On ghosts, [A3] gives $s^2=0$, BRST square $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)$, and mixed square $\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$. Adding the three terms yields the same constant-gauge plus circle symmetry. [A3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The element $a_0$ is a constant gauge parameter and $\\omega$ generates the selected smooth circle isometry of $S^4_r$. Thus the square is a gauge transformation plus an isometry on the fields. The induced circle action on $S^4_r$ satisfies the compact-group and smooth-action hypotheses in [F2], so its ordinary geometric Cartan complex is defined. This use is only on $S^4_r$ and does not transfer the finite-dimensional definition to the field configuration space. [A1, A2, A3, F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1]\n\n5.1 The adopted contour, measure, and S4 regulator extension are explicit formal inputs in [A4]; the verification above neither constructs them nor proves a regulator-removal or $t\\to\\infty$ limit. [A4, step 4.1] ∎"
