## Verification

1.1 For an adjoint-valued tensor field $X$, expand $(s+\delta)^2X=s^2X+\{s,\delta\}X+\delta^2X$. By [A1], $s^2X=sX'=\delta_{\mathrm{gauge}}(\phi)X+\delta_{U(1)}(\omega)X$. By [A3], the gauge term cancels with its negative in $\{s,\delta\}X$, leaving $\delta_{\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)X+\delta_{U(1)}(\omega)X$. [A1, A3, given, algebra]

2.1 For the connection, the same square expansion applies to $A$ and $\psi$. By [A1], $s^2A=s\psi=\delta_{\mathrm{gauge}}(\phi)A+\delta_{U(1)}(\omega)A$; by [A2] and [A3], its BRST rule is $\delta A_\mu=D_\mu c=-\delta_{\mathrm{gauge}}(c)A_\mu$, its mixed term cancels the $\phi$ action, and its BRST square is $\delta_{\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)A$. Thus $Q^2A=\delta_{\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)A+\delta_{U(1)}(\omega)A$, using the declared connection gauge action $-D_\mu u$. [A1, A2, A3, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 On ghosts, [A3] gives $s^2=0$, BRST square $\delta_{\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)$, and mixed square $\delta_{U(1)}(\omega)$. Adding the three terms yields the same constant-gauge plus circle symmetry. [A3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 The element $a_0$ is a constant gauge parameter and $\omega$ generates the selected smooth circle isometry of $S^4_r$. Thus the square is a gauge transformation plus an isometry on the fields. The induced circle action on $S^4_r$ satisfies the compact-group and smooth-action hypotheses in [F2], so its ordinary geometric Cartan complex is defined. This use is only on $S^4_r$ and does not transfer the finite-dimensional definition to the field configuration space. [A1, A2, A3, F1, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 3.1]

5.1 The adopted contour, measure, and S4 regulator extension are explicit formal inputs in [A4]; the verification above neither constructs them nor proves a regulator-removal or $t\to\infty$ limit. [A4, step 4.1] ∎
[F2] [[thm-associated-vector-bundle-is-well-defined]]: for a smooth right
principal bundle and a smooth finite-dimensional real representation, this
quotient has a smooth real vector-bundle structure whose transition maps are
the representation matrices of the principal transition functions.

## Proof

1.1 The underlying real space $W_{\mathbb R}$ is finite-dimensional. In a
complex basis, the inclusion $\mathrm{GL}_{\mathbb C}(W)\hookrightarrow
\mathrm{GL}_{\mathbb R}(W_{\mathbb R})$ sends a matrix $A$ to the real block
matrix $\begin{pmatrix}\operatorname{Re}A&-\operatorname{Im}A\\
\operatorname{Im}A&\operatorname{Re}A\end{pmatrix}$; its entries depend
linearly on the real and imaginary parts of those of $A$, so the inclusion is
smooth. Therefore $\rho_{\mathbb R}$ is a smooth real representation.
Applying [F2] supplies the smooth real vector-bundle structure and the local charts
induced by sections $s_i:U_i\to P$, with transition maps $\rho_{\mathbb R}(g_{ji})$.
[F2, given, algebra]

